    {
      "source": "hackernews",
      "subsource": "news.ycombinator.com",
      "category": "ai",
      "title": "Publish fake stats, get cited in LLMs",
      "url": "https://peec.ai/blog/publish-stats-get-cited-the-tactic-that-put-my-data-all-over-the-web",
      "discuss_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50165801",
      "score": 294,
      "comments": 166,
      "author": "robtherobber",
      "published": "2026-10-20T13:06:22Z",
      "bullets": [
        "LLMs and search-grounded models ingest unverified web data, allowing fabricated statistics to penetrate RAG pipelines and training corpora.",
        "Manipulating targeted SEO allows adversaries to easily poison citations returned by tools like Perplexity, Bing Copilot, and Google AI Overviews.",
        "Reliable RAG architectures require automated fact-checking, domain-reputation filtering, and multi-source consensus verification rather than relying on raw web indexers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "In Context",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5eO7BNyJao",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/b36ixw",
      "score": 34,
      "comments": 10,
      "author": "veqq",
      "published": "2026-10-19T18:25:48Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Pass explicit context instances down call trees to reliably propagate cancellation signals, deadlines, and timeouts across network boundaries.",
        "Avoid using context value stores as implicit parameter bags; restrict storage to cross-cutting concerns like trace IDs and auth tokens.",
        "Structured concurrency models offer safer alternatives to ad-hoc context propagation by tying asynchronous task lifecycles to lexical execution scopes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/security",
      "category": "security",
      "title": "Protecting Firefox users following the compromise of eMudhra intermediate certificates",
      "url": "https://blog.mozilla.org/security/2026/10/20/protecting-firefox-users-following-the-compromise-of-emudhra-intermediate-certificates/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/gqyvn5",
      "score": 94,
      "comments": 41,
      "author": "freddyb",
      "published": "2026-10-20T13:00:50Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Compromised intermediate CA private keys at Indian CA eMudhra exposed users to potential TLS man-in-the-middle spoofing attacks.",
        "Mozilla blocked affected subordinate certificates remotely across Firefox clients using OneCRL and CRLite push-revocation mechanisms.",
        "Security teams should verify OS and enterprise trust stores, ensuring rogue eMudhra intermediates are revoked and Certificate Transparency logs monitored."
      ]
    },
